<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Middle East</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Middle East is the region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. It has been the setting of many empires, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> and Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphates</a> to the Seljuk, Safavid and Ottoman states. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>'s invasions reshaped the region, and modern powers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> also influenced its politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/middle-east.jpg" alt="Middle East" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TownDown · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Middle_East_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Afsharid Empire's impact on the political geography of the <b>Middle East</b> and South Asia was disproportionate to its brief span of eleven years as a great power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Soba was a trading hub on north-south and east-west routes, and goods reached the kingdom from Makuria, the <b>Middle East</b>, western Africa, India and China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ayyubid Empire governed the most economically productive region of the medieval <b>Middle East</b>, spanning Egypt, sustained by the annual Nile flood, and the commercial centres of Syria, the Levantine coast and Upper Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Politically, they created the pattern of a secular sultan ruling in the caliph's name, which the Seljuks inherited, and they spread the iqta as the basis of military finance across the <b>Middle East</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Circassia was the homeland of the Adyghe peoples, a stateless country of princes and free communities that supplied Egypt with Mamluk sultans, resisted Russian expansion for a century, and was destroyed in 1864 by conquest and mass deportation, creating a diaspora that remains across the <b>Middle East</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan's defeat of Russia in 1905 reverberated across Asia, Africa, and the <b>Middle East</b> as the first modern demonstration that European military supremacy was not inevitable, encouraging nationalists from India to Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
